## Shades That Disappoint: What to Skip and Why

Not avoiding these is the reason a manicure looks off when the colour seemed right in the bottle.

**Grey-based nudes and beiges**: they look like skin-tone-adjacent neutrals in the display but read cold and flat on dark skin. The grey base has nowhere to go  it neither contrasts nor harmonises, it just sits there looking like the nails need a second coat.

**Sheer pastels**: addressed above, but worth repeating. Sheer formula plus deep pigmentation equals optical mixing, and the result is almost never the colour that was in the bottle. Baby pink, milky yellow, and translucent lavender are the main offenders.

**Orange-adjacent reds on cool skin**: a warm red with strong orange in it on a cool-toned dark complexion can read clashing rather than vibrant. It is not that warm reds do not work on cool dark skin  it is that the undertone gap becomes visible. A blue-red or true red sits more comfortably.

**Lavender with grey or white in the base**: these look beautiful in photographs taken on lighter hands and turn chalky on dark skin. Look for lavenders described as "deep" or "rich," not "dusty" or "soft." <a href='https://www.lorealparisusa.com/beauty-magazine/beauty-tips/beauty-trends/nail-colors-for-dark-skin' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>L'Oréal's nail colour edit</a> identifies seventeen shades that consistently work across deeper skin tones, and the pattern holds  pigment depth is the recurring factor. <a href='https://lovecnd.com/blogs/news/which-nail-polish-colour-suits-dark-skin' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>CND's dark skin nail guide</a> and <a href='https://us.manucurist.com/blogs/journal/best-nail-polish-colors-for-dark-skin' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>Manucurist's colour theory guide</a> both confirm the same principle: formula opacity is as important as shade selection.

