## How to Choose the Right Finish (Gloss, Matte, Chrome) for Your Skin Depth

The finish is not the afterthought. On dark skin especially, finish changes the entire reading of a colour  the same shade in gloss versus matte can feel like two completely different nails.

**Gloss** is the default for dark skin, and for good reason. A glossy finish catches light and amplifies whatever richness is already in the colour. On deep complexions, it gives nails a depth and dimension that matte cannot replicate. Cherry red with a high-gloss finish on ebony skin looks lacquered and intentional. The same red in matte looks editorial but also requires perfect application to avoid looking flat.

**Matte** works best on dark skin in two situations: deep, rich colours (burgundy, oxblood, forest green, deep plum) where the matte finish adds a velvety quality that feels luxurious rather than flat, and when the shape of the nail is doing enough visual work that the finish can stay quiet. Matte on light or pastel colours on dark skin tends to read chalky  the combination of low-pigment colour and light-absorbing finish removes all the visual weight.

**Chrome and metallic finishes** behave differently on dark skin than on any other complexion, because the depth of the canvas gives the chrome something to reflect against. A chrome powder over a dark base on deep skin produces a mirror quality that is genuinely striking. Gold chrome, rose gold chrome, and holographic finishes are all amplified by the contrast with the skin in a way that does not happen on lighter complexions. For shape-specific guidance on how finish interacts with nail shape on darker skin, the breakdown of <a href='https://mirelleinspo.com/blog/almond-nail-colors-skin-tone' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>almond nail colours for every skin tone</a> is worth reading alongside this.

